namespace Pscf {
namespace Rp {

/*! 
\page rp_BdMove_page BdMove

A BdMove implements a MC move that performs a short BD simulation, 
and always accepts the result. Unlike other MC moves, this move does 
not exactly satisfy detailed balance, and so incurs a systematic 
error similar to that found in pure BD simulations.

The BdMove class is provided to allow users to mix BD and MC 
sampling strategies. For large systems, BD simulation is generally 
more efficient than MC simulation, because the acceptance rates of
the types of MC moves used in field theoretic simulations generally
decreases with increasing system size.  By combining a BdMove with 
a one or more more specialized MC move, a user may combine the 
efficiency of BD simulation for large systems with specialized
capabilities provided by another type of MC move. 

\see Rp::BdMove (class API)

\section rp_BdMove_algorithm_sec Step Algorithm

Each step of the BdMove algorithm runs a short BD simulation, using
the Leimkuler-Matthews BD step algorithm that is also used by the
\ref rp_LMBdStep_page BD step algorithm.

\section rp_BdMove_parameter_sec Parameter file

The only parameters needed by this algorithm are the "probability" 
parameter, which determines the probability that a move of this type
will be attempted, a "mobility" parameter, which is the mobility 
parameter of the BD step algorithm, and parameter "nStep", which
specifieds the number of BD steps that should be taken per MC move
of this type.  All three parameters are required. A typical example 
of an associated parameter file block is shown below.
\code
  BdMove{
    probability   0.5
    mobility      0.005
    nStep         100
  }
\endcode
The format for this block is thus:
\code
  BdMove{
    probability  float (in range [0,1])
    mobility     float
    nStep        int
  }
\endcode
The meanings of all parameters are also summarized briefly below:
<table>
  <tr>
    <td> <b> Label </b>  </td>
    <td> <b> Description </b>  </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td> probability </td>
    <td> probability of attempting a step of this type  </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td> mobility </td>
    <td> Mobility parameter of the underlying BD step algorith </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td> nStep </td>
    <td> number of BD steps per MC move of this type </td>
  </tr>
</table>

*/

}
}
